# GrowthOS

Build GrowthOS as a multi-tenant SaaS AI marketing agency. Each client organization has an isolated workspace and must never access another organization's data.

## Required Stack

- Next.js 15 App Router, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, and shadcn/ui
- Supabase Postgres, Auth, Storage, Row Level Security, and Realtime
- Anthropic Claude API for all agent work; default to `claude-sonnet-4-6` and allow per-task model overrides
- Trigger.dev or Inngest for background, scheduled, and long-running work
- Resend for transactional email; connected ESPs for client campaigns
- Stripe for billing and Vercel for deployment
- Do not deviate from this stack without asking

## Architecture

- Multi-tenancy is mandatory. Every tenant-owned table must have `organization_id` and Postgres RLS policies in the same migration that creates it.
- All Supabase queries must run through RLS and must never return cross-tenant data.
- Roles are `platform_admin`, `org_owner`, `org_admin`, `org_member`, and `org_viewer`.
- Run all AI work as background jobs with `queued`, `running`, `complete`, and `failed` states, streamed logs, and persisted results.
- Send every externally publishable AI output to an approvals queue. Publish only after explicit approval or when the organization has enabled auto-publish for that channel.
- Store external API credentials encrypted per organization, never in code or environment variables for client accounts.
- Put each module in a separate route group under `/app/(dashboard)/[module]`.
- Prefer Server Components; use Client Components only when interactivity requires them.
- Store money as integer minor units with a currency column.

## Code Quality

- Enable TypeScript strict mode. Avoid `any`; comment the reason when unavoidable.
- Validate every API input and structured AI output with Zod.
- Put every database change in a numbered SQL file under `/supabase/migrations`.
- Create RLS policies in the same migration as their table.
- Keep components under approximately 200 lines and extract logic to `/lib`.
- After each feature, summarize what was built and what requires manual testing.
- A fix is not done until it is merged to `main`, deployed, and verified on production. Never report a fix as complete while it exists only on a feature branch. At the end of any task, state which branch the work is on and whether production has it.
- "Deployed" means the Vercel **production** deployment is **READY** and its commit hash includes the change. A merged PR with a failed Vercel build is **NOT** shipped. After every push to `main`, check deployment status (GitHub `Vercel` status / `gh api repos/.../commits/<sha>/status`) and do not claim shipped until `state=success` and Ready. Always report the live production commit hash.
- Before claiming a code change is shippable, run the exact production build locally (`npm run build`) and confirm exit code 0.
- A fix involving migrations is not done until the migrations are applied to production and verified by querying prod — deploying app code without its migrations is a broken deploy. The merge-deploy-verify checklist includes the database.

## AI Agents

- Keep versioned agent prompts in `/lib/agents/prompts`; never define prompts inline.
- Give agents the organization's brand guidelines, brand voice, target audience, and relevant research documents.
- Require structured JSON output, validate it with Zod, and only then persist it.
- Log every run to `agent_runs`, including organization, input/output tokens, cost, duration, and model.
- Agents cover brand and competitor research, brand identity, social, email, paid ads, SEO, planning, CRM, finance, operations, compliance, analytics, workflows, and AI-run meetings and reviews.
